Now I can see the full picture. The only types from `types.ts` that are actually imported by consumers are:

1. `AirborneEntry` - imported by `apps/adsb-service/src/index.ts`
2. `TimerState` - imported by `apps/fr24-scraper/src/index.ts` and `packages/database/scheduler.ts`

All other types defined in `types.ts` (including `PushNotification`, `ServiceError`, `ValidationError`, `ConfigError`, `FlightIdentifier`, etc.) are NOT imported by any consumer — they're defined but unused.

The `AirborneEntry` interface is:
```typescript
export interface AirborneEntry {
  flightId: number;
  arrivalAirport: string;
  missedPolls: number;
}
```
This is pure data. It's used locally as a Map value in `adsb-service` to track which flights are airborne. Data comes from database queries and ADS-B API responses. No user input.

The `TimerState` interface is:
```typescript
export interface TimerState {
  scrapeTimeout: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null;
  wakeTimeout: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null;
  prefetchSlotTimeout?: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null;
  tzCheckTimeout?: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null;
}
```
This is just timer handles. Used with `clearTimeout()` and `setTimeout()`. No vulnerability.

The key concern I had — `PushNotification.data: Record<string, unknown>` — is moot because `PushNotification` is never used anywhere. The notification service builds its own payload inline.

Similarly, `ServiceError` and its subclasses with `details?: unknown` are never used. Even if they were, they just store data — there's no dynamic code execution, no template rendering, no eval.

This file is definitively clean. There is no vulnerability anchored on `packages/common/src/types.ts`.

Let me write the no-finding report.

